course: history
lesson: 3
title: Mesopotamia: the first cities and writing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mesopotamia means the land between the rivers: the Tigris and the Euphrates, in what is now mostly Iraq. There, about 5,000 years ago, some of the first real cities in the world grew up.
explain: The land was dry, so farmers dug canals to bring river water to their fields. More food meant bigger towns, and cities like Uruk had tens of thousands of people.
explain: With so much grain and so many sheep to count, people needed a way to remember who owned what, like a shop writing down what you owe. So the Sumerians invented writing, first as little pictures of grain and sheep.
explain: Scribes pressed a cut reed into wet clay, making wedge-shaped marks called cuneiform. Children went to school to learn it, and some of their practice tablets still survive, mistakes and all.
explain: Over the city rose a ziggurat, a stepped tower of mud brick, and the Sumerians wrote down stories like the Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest stories in the world.
fact: Mesopotamia means the land between the rivers, the Tigris and the Euphrates, in what is now mostly Iraq.
fact: Uruk, about 5,000 years ago, had tens of thousands of people, big temples and city walls, and is one of the first real cities in the world.
fact: Farmers dug canals to carry river water to their fields, which is called irrigation.
fact: The Sumerians invented one of the first writing systems about 5,200 years ago; it began as little pictures used to count grain and sheep.
fact: Scribes pressed a cut reed into wet clay, making wedge-shaped marks; the word cuneiform means wedge-shaped. They had no paper, but river clay was everywhere.
fact: Boys, and a few girls, learned to write at tablet-houses, and some practice tablets survive with mistakes and complaints about strict teachers.
fact: Each city had a ziggurat, a huge stepped tower of mud brick with a temple on top for the city's god.
fact: The Bible's story of the Tower of Babel was probably inspired by a real ziggurat in Babylon.
fact: The Sumerians counted in groups of sixty, which is why we still have 60 seconds in a minute and 60 minutes in an hour.
fact: The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest stories ever written down: a king loses his best friend Enkidu, searches for a way to live for ever and learns that all people die.
fact: Gilgamesh includes a great flood in which a man builds a big boat, takes animals on board and sends out birds to find land.
fact: Many peoples tell flood stories, and the rivers of Mesopotamia really did flood badly.
exercise: What does the name Mesopotamia mean? | The land between the rivers | The land of the great pyramids | The land of ice | The land beyond the sea
exercise: Which two rivers flow through Mesopotamia? | The Tigris and the Euphrates | The Nile and the Congo | The Thames and the Seine in Europe | The Indus and the Ganges
exercise: In which modern country is most of Mesopotamia? | Iraq | Egypt | Greece | India
exercise: About how many people lived in Uruk 5,000 years ago? | Tens of thousands | About fifty | Ten million | Exactly one hundred
exercise: What is irrigation? | Carrying water to fields along canals | Building thick walls around a whole city | Counting sheep with pictures | Climbing a stepped tower
exercise: Why was writing first invented in Mesopotamia? | To count and record grain and sheep | To write love letters to kings | To print story books for the children | To send text messages
exercise: About how long ago did the Sumerians invent writing? | About 5,200 years ago | About 520 years ago | About 52,000 years ago | About 52 years ago
exercise: What did scribes write on? | Wet clay | Paper from a shop | Smooth glass | Silk cloth
exercise: What does the word cuneiform mean? | Wedge-shaped | Picture of a bird | Holy writing | Written by a king
exercise: What did pupils at the tablet-houses complain about on their tablets? | Strict teachers | Too many holidays | Bad school dinners on phones | Football results
exercise: What was a ziggurat? | A stepped tower of mud brick with a temple on top | A royal tomb shaped like a smooth pyramid of white stone | A wide canal for boats | A big market square
exercise: Which Bible story was probably inspired by a ziggurat? | The Tower of Babel | Noah's ark | Jonah and the whale | David and Goliath
exercise: The Sumerians counted in groups of how many? | Sixty | Ten | Twelve | A hundred
exercise: What was Gilgamesh looking for? | A way to live for ever | A lost golden crown | A new river for his city | A wife from Egypt
exercise: What happens in the flood story in Gilgamesh? | A man builds a boat, takes animals and sends out birds | A whole city is pulled up into the sky by angry gods | A king swims across the sea | The rivers dry up for ever
test: Why are there 60 minutes in an hour and not 100? | The Sumerians counted in sixties and we kept it | The Romans had sixty fingers | A computer chose sixty in 1990 | Sixty is the biggest number there is, so it was the best one to use
test: lol why didn't they just use paper? | They had no paper, but clay was everywhere | Paper was banned by the kings, who hated it | They thought paper was too boring | They used paper but it all burned
test: What would happen to Uruk's fields if all the canals filled with mud? | The crops would dry up and the city go hungry | The fields would grow more wheat than ever | Nothing, it rained there every day | The river would flow uphill instead
test: A friend says writing began with poems and stories. What really came first? | Lists counting grain and sheep | Long poems about the gods | Letters to friends far away | Road signs for carts
test: A school tablet 4,000 years old has a mistake on it. What does that show? | Children learning to write made mistakes like us | Writing was never taught to anyone, so they guessed | The teacher could not write at all | Clay cannot hold writing well
test: Why did Mesopotamian farmers need canals but jungle farmers might not? | Mesopotamia was dry and needed river water | Jungle plants do not need water | Canals were only for fun | Mesopotamia had too much rain
test: My cousin says Gilgamesh is a Marvel hero. What is he really? | A king in one of the oldest written stories | A Viking god of thunder with a magic hammer | A Roman emperor | A Chinese inventor
test: Why do you think many peoples tell flood stories? | Real floods were big, scary events for river peoples | Every land had the same king | Someone sent one book round the whole world long ago | Floods never happened anywhere
test: If you found wedge-shaped marks on an old piece of clay, what are they probably? | Cuneiform writing | Bird footprints by chance | Hieroglyphs from Egypt | Chinese characters
test: Gilgamesh learns that all people die. What does this show about people 4,000 years ago? | They worried about friendship and death like us | They did not care about anything | They thought nobody ever died | They had no stories at all
exam: Identify the two rivers between which the region designated Mesopotamia is situated. | The Tigris and the Euphrates | The Nile and the Jordan | The Indus and the Ganges | The Danube and the Rhine
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the original purpose of Sumerian writing? | The recording of quantities of grain and livestock | The composition of epic poetry | The correspondence of private persons | The proclamation of royal laws
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term cuneiform. | It signifies wedge-shaped | It signifies sacred carving | It signifies picture of a bird | It signifies written upon papyrus
exam: Determine the base of the system of numeration employed by the Sumerians, whereof traces persist in the measurement of time. | Sixty | Ten | Twenty | Twelve
